VI. TERMINATION

  This License may be terminated only as expressly provided in this Section VI.
  The Copyright Holder may not revoke the License at its discretion for users
  who remain in compliance with its terms.

  (a) Notice and cure. If you breach this License, the Copyright Holder may
      terminate it only after giving you written notice describing the breach
      with reasonable particularity and allowing a cure period of thirty (30)
      days (or longer if stated in the notice). If you cure the breach within
      that period, this License does not terminate by reason of that notice.

  (b) Effect of termination. If this License terminates under Section VI(a)
      because the breach was not cured in time, then, except as provided in
      Section VI(c), you must cease new use of the Software and destroy copies
      in your possession that are not covered by Section VI(c).

  (c) Grandfathered Permitted Uses. Termination does not require you to
      recall, disable, or rebuild:
        (i)   websites, applications, or other products already delivered to
              a client or end users under a Permitted Use before termination;
        (ii)  production deployments already live under a Permitted Use before
              termination; or
        (iii) copies of a specific Software version you lawfully obtained and
              embedded in such delivered or published work before termination.

      For those grandfathered artifacts only, the rights needed to keep them
      running under a Permitted Use continue on a perpetual, royalty-free basis
      even after termination, and do not require a new license grant. Ordinary
      maintenance of those artifacts is included and may cover bug fixes,
      security updates to dependencies, rebuilds for updated toolchains (for
      example a newer Node.js or bundler), and other changes reasonably
      necessary to keep an already delivered or already live project operating
      as previously shipped. That maintenance right does not authorize new
      projects, new distributions, or using the Software to start work that was
      not already covered under Section VI(c)(i)–(iii) before termination.

  (d) Survival. Sections IV, V, VI, and VII survive termination.


