FAQ (informative only; does not modify the License)
---------------------------------------------------

  Q: Can I use upwwwards in a paid client website or SaaS product UI?
  A: Yes — that is a Permitted Use, including commercial projects.

  Q: Can AI tools generate code that calls upwwwards APIs?
  A: Yes. Generating integration code for a Permitted Use is fine. Shipping a
     Competitive Product or republishing the Software source is not.

  Q: If the License is terminated, do my client sites have to go offline?
  A: No. Section VI(c) grandfathers delivered and already-published Permitted
     Uses (and the Software versions embedded in them). Termination stops new
     use and new projects, after notice and an uncured breach.

  Q: Can I patch or fork the Software for my own app?
  A: Yes. Section II allows private forks and modifications for internal use or
     a Permitted Use (including shipping those mods inside a client deployment).
     Publishing a public fork or alternate package is not allowed.

  Q: Must client sites show upwwwards branding?
  A: No. Section III(f) keeps copyright/license notices in the distribution
     where practicable, but does not require UI trademarks or branding.

  Q: Can my npm package (e.g. my-framework) depend on upwwwards?
  A: Yes. Section II allows distributing the Software as a dependency of your
     own package so installs pull the official upwwwards package, provided it
     enables a Permitted Use, is not a Competitive Product, and is not a
     modified or rebranded standalone republish of the Software.

  Q: If my package is MIT (or another OSS license), does that open-source
     upwwwards?
  A: No. Section II(d) is clear: recipients of your package are licensed for
     the Software only under this License, not under broader terms of your
     package. Publishing an MIT (or similar) wrapper that depends on upwwwards
     does not make upwwwards open source; your users must still comply with
     this License for the Software itself.

  Q: Is the license perpetual for my client’s shipped site?
  A: For grandfathered delivered / already-live Permitted Uses, Section VI(c)
     keeps the rights needed to run those artifacts perpetual and royalty-free,
     even if your License later terminates. That does not cover new projects
     after termination.

  Q: What counts as a Competitive Product?
  A: A package that mainly reproduces upwwwards’ functionality and is marketed
     as a substitute can be competitive. A package that depends on upwwwards to
     add complementary features (for example a larger site framework, router,
     or UI toolkit that uses upwwwards for page lifecycle / animation) is not
     automatically a Competitive Product solely for that reason.

  Q: What is allowed at a glance?
  A: Use in client/commercial sites: yes. Private patches for your project: yes.
     npm package depending on upwwwards: yes. Distributed modified fork: no.
     Competing / rebranded standalone package: no.

  Q: Is this MIT / Apache / GPL?
  A: No. This is a proprietary no-charge standard-use license.